{
  "image_name": "3d7df844d5d26ea43566e6b30bbf4f4d.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how the kidney glomerulus is built and how its main cell types communicate to maintain or damage the filtration barrier. The upper part shows the glomerular tuft between the afferent and efferent arterioles, enclosed by Bowman capsule and connected to the proximal tubule. A magnified cross-section highlights the layered filtration interface: podocyte foot processes on the urinary side, the glomerular basement membrane (GBM) in the middle, and glomerular endothelial cells lining the vascular compartment, with a glycocalyx coating the endothelial surface. Together these layers form the barrier that filters blood.\n\nThe lower part focuses on signaling between podocytes and glomerular endothelial cells (GECs). Several pathways appear protective or regulatory: podocyte-derived VEGFA acts on VEGFR2 in endothelial cells, endothelin/EDNRB signaling promotes nitric oxide–mediated vasodilation, and ANGPT1 acting through the TEK receptor supports endothelial survival and barrier maintenance. In contrast, signals from endothelial cells back to podocytes seem injurious: APC, TGFβ1, and nitric oxide/free diffusion are linked to mitochondrial pathway suppression, podocyte apoptosis, and peroxynitrite-mediated podocyte injury. Overall, the figure emphasizes that balanced bidirectional crosstalk is essential for normal glomerular function.",
